  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>The Baou de Saint-Jeannet is one of the most recognisable summits inland from Nice: a rocky plateau rising to 802 metres that towers over the village of the same name. The climb alternates between a scrubland path, stretches between pines and a few rocky sections, before opening onto a vast summit plateau with an orientation table and a 360° panorama over the French Riviera, from Cap d'Antibes to the first foothills of the Préalpes.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>The marked trail is manageable for most regular hikers, with around 600 metres of elevation gain on the full loop from Saint-Jeannet; some sources rate it as moderate, others as more demanding depending on the variant taken, with a somewhat trickier passage just before the summit. Dogs are allowed on the trail but must be kept on a leash, notably because of busy stretches and the presence of livestock in places. The site is very busy at weekends: it's best to set off early, especially in summer when the heat can be intense on the exposed, shadeless sections.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: bring plenty of water for your dog — water points are scarce along the route, and the summit plateau is very exposed to the sun.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The hilltop village of Saint-Jeannet, at the foot of the Baou, is worth a detour with its typical lanes and views over the Var valley. La Gaude, a neighbouring village, offers other hiking routes on its own Baou.</p>
    </div>

    <aside>
      <div class="sidebar-card">
        <h3>Worth checking on-site</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><svg><use href="#i-alert"/></svg> Access to forested areas is restricted when wildfire risk is high (mid-June to mid-September) — check the departmental map before setting off.</li>
          <li><svg><use href="#i-alert"/></svg> The rocky passage (the "voie rouge") below the summit is manageable going up on good rock, but explicitly discouraged for down-climbing — confirmed by 3 specialised hiking/climbing sources. Come back down via a different route rather than the same passage.</li>
          <li><svg><use href="#i-check"/></svg> Little shade on the summit plateau — avoid the hottest hours in summer.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
